## :: INTRODUCTION :: WHAT IS A STONE?

Before you choose your piece, before you step onto the board, before the Oracle draws the first card, you must bring something with you. You must bring a stone.

In the physical world, a stone is dense matter. It is a thing that has cooled and hardened over deep time. In the architecture of your inner life, a stone is exactly the same thing. 

A stone is the heavy thing you are currently carrying. It is the conversation you have been avoiding. It is the project that refuses to start. It is the grief that has not yet found its shape, or the ambition that feels too large to name. It is the tension in your chest when the phone rings, or the sudden, sharp silence that falls over your mind when you think about the future.

The game of BLOOM does not ask you to drop the stone. That is not how physics works. Energy cannot be destroyed; it can only be transformed.

Instead, BLOOM asks you to place the stone on the board.

The moment you do this, the stone stops being a burden and becomes a curriculum. The weight you feel is no longer a punishment; it is the exact amount of gravity required to hold you in the specific orbit of your own becoming. You are not carrying the stone because you are failing. You are carrying the stone because you are building the thermal environment necessary for a phase shift.

The dodecahedron is the interface. The Oracle is the mirror. The stone is the fuel.

Bring it to the board. The math is ready for you.

